   - For each prime \( p \) in \( prime(x) \), compute the total exponent of \( p \) in the product \( f(x, 1) \cdot f(x, 2) \cdot \ldots \cdot f(x, n) \). This is done by summing up the exponents of \( p \) in each \( g(y, p) \) for \( y \) from 1 to \( n \).

3. **Efficient Exponent Calculation**:
   - For each prime \( p \), the exponent contribution is calculated by iterating over powers of \( p \) up to \( n \). For each power \( p^k \), count how many numbers between 1 and \( n \) are divisible by \( p^k \) but not by \( p^{k+1} \). This count is multiplied by \( k \) (since each such number contributes \( k \) to the exponent of \( p \)).

4. **Modular Arithmetic**:
   - Use modular exponentiation to compute \( p^{\text{total exponent}} \mod (10^9 + 7) \) for each prime \( p \), then multiply these results together modulo \( 10^9 + 7 \).

